1
00:00:00,130 --> 00:00:01,730
Has this ever happened?

2
00:00:02,075 --> 00:00:04,435
Shuffle a deck,
and look at the order.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:07,765
The first card
can be any of 52,

4
00:00:08,085 --> 00:00:10,025
the next any
of the rest.

5
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:12,295
Multiply all
those choices,

6
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:13,905
and you count
the orders.

7
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,240
The answer
has 68 digits,

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,225
too big to say out loud.

9
00:00:19,590 --> 00:00:20,865
Each shuffle is just

10
00:00:20,865 --> 00:00:23,175
one pick
from that huge pile.

11
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:28,145
Now give everyone alive
a deck, 8 billion of us.

12
00:00:28,165 --> 00:00:32,055
Shuffle once a second
for 14 billion years,

13
00:00:32,055 --> 00:00:33,660
the age of the universe.

14
00:00:36,325 --> 00:00:39,520
Even then, you cover
less than one part

15
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:42,925
in a trillion trillion
trillion of the orders.

16
00:00:43,345 --> 00:00:45,445
Your shuffle
is almost certainly

17
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,180
the first of its kind.

18
00:00:50,725 --> 00:00:53,120
Give the planet
1,000 times more

19
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:55,590
shufflers,
and it barely changes.

20
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,660
The pile of orders
is just too big.

21
00:00:59,730 --> 00:01:01,920
Shuffle well,
and your order has

22
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:05,440
almost certainly
never existed before.

23
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,155
Not certain,
but a very safe bet.

24
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:09,920
Next envelope:

25
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:12,885
all the water on Earth,
in one ball.
